'''Richard Lee Jackson''' (born March 16, 1954)<ref name="smn-26">{{cite news |last1=Countryman |first1=Vanessa |title=Georgia billionaire enters crowded governor race. Who is Rick Jackson? |url=https://www.savannahnow.com/story/news/2026/02/05/who-is-rick-jackson-ga-meet-the-millionaire-entering-governors-race/88524864007/ |work=Savannah Morning News |date=February 5, 2026}}</ref> is an American billionaire businessman and politician. He is the founder and CEO of Jackson Healthcare, a healthcare staffing and services company, in [[Alpharetta, Georgia]].<ref name="JH-bio">{{cite web |title=Richard L. Jackson |website=Jackson Healthcare |url=https://jacksonhealthcare.com/team-members/richard-l-jackson/ |access-date=February 7, 2026}}</ref> 

After defeating [[Lieutenant Governor of Georgia|lieutenant governor]] [[Burt Jones]] in the [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican]] primary runoff, he became the Republican nominee in the [[2026 Georgia gubernatorial election]] and will face Democratic nominee [[Keisha Lance Bottoms]] in the general election.<ref name="AP-announcement">{{cite news |last=Amy |first=Jeff |title=Business owner Rick Jackson enters Georgia GOP governor's race |work=Associated Press |date=February 3, 2026 |url=https://apnews.com/article/rick-jackson-republican-governor-georgia-jones-trump-66a33ab0de0c92cdf5ccc50f26ef7418 |access-date=February 7, 2026}}</ref><ref name="smn-26" /><ref>{{Cite web |date=October 7, 2026 |title=Georgia governor's debate highlights voters' top concerns |url=https://www.pbs.org/newshour/show/georgia-governors-debate-highlights-voterss-top-concerns |access-date= |website=PBS}}</ref>

==Early life and education==
Richard Lee Jackson was born on March 16, 1954, and grew up in [[Midtown Atlanta]]. He described his childhood as being marked by poverty and, later, time in the foster care system.<ref name="gabiz-24"/><ref name="AJC-profile">{{cite web |title=Atlanta's Rick Jackson, from foster child to philanthropist |website=The Atlanta Journal-Constitution |url=https://specials.myajc.com/self-made-philanthropist/ |date=November 22, 2015 |access-date=February 7, 2026}}</ref><ref name="jackson-speech-2014">{{cite web |last1=Jackson |first1=Rick |title=Never a Better Time to Transform Georgia Child Welfare |url=https://www.georgiapolicy.org/news/never-a-better-time-to-transform-georgia-child-welfare/ |website=Georgia Public Policy Foundation |access-date=13 March 2026 |date=November 14, 2013}}</ref> He attended [[Lipscomb University]] in [[Nashville]] before dropping out.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Rick Jackson |url=https://www.forbes.com/profile/rick-jackson/ |access-date=October 13, 2026 |website=Forbes |language=en}}</ref>

==Business career==
Jackson founded his first medical recruitment business in 1978.<ref name="gabiz-24"/>

In 2012, Jackson, with businessmen Michael Kendrick and Larry Powell, purchased [[Family Christian Stores]] (FRS) from [[Madison Dearborn Partners]].<ref name="pw-garrett-12">{{cite web |last1=Garrett |first1=Lynn |title=Family Christian Stores in Management, Investors Buyout |url=https://www.publishersweekly.com/pw/by-topic/industry-news/religion/article/54789-family-christian-stores-in-management-investors-buyout.html |publisher=Publisher Weekly |access-date=13 March 2026 |date=November 16, 2012}}</ref> The company was converted to a nonprofit and made a subsidiary of Family Christian Resource Centers (FCRC), controlled by Jackson. A 2015 plan by Jackson to file for [[Chapter 11, Title 11, United States Code|Chapter 11 bankruptcy]] was opposed by the [[United States Trustee Program|U.S. Trustee Office]] and initially rejected by a bankruptcy judge.<ref name="freep-2015">{{cite news |last1=Bomey |first1=Nathan |title=Family Christian books bankruptcy: Chain faces scrutiny |url=https://www.freep.com/story/money/business/michigan/2015/04/18/family-christian-bankruptcy-bookstore-chain/25531487/ |work=[[The Detroit Free Press]] |date=April 8, 2015}}</ref><ref name="wng-15">{{cite news |last1=Langdon |first1=Lynde |title=Judge rejects another Family Christian Stores bankruptcy plan |url=https://wng.org/sift/judge-rejects-another-family-christian-stores-bankruptcy-plan-1617252280 |work=World News Group |date=June 22, 2015}}</ref><ref name="mlive-15">{{cite news |last1=Herger |first1=Jim |title=Family Christian Stores given new life after bankruptcy judge approves sale |url=https://www.mlive.com/business/west-michigan/2015/08/family_christian_stores_celebr.html |work=mlive.com |date=August 11, 2015}}</ref>

Jackson co-established Jackson Acquisitions, a [[special-purpose acquisition company]] (SPAC), with [[Jeb Bush]] in 2021; Bush was chairman and Jackson was CEO.<ref name="rjacu-21">{{cite web |title=Jeb Bush's SPAC Jackson Acquisition prices $200 million IPO |url=https://www.renaissancecapital.com/IPO-Center/News/89371/Jeb-Bushs-SPAC-Jackson-Acquisition-prices-$200-million-IPO |access-date=13 March 2026 |date=December 9, 2021}}</ref> They dissolved the SPAC in 2023.<ref name="spac-news-23">{{cite web |title=Jeb Bush’s Jackson Acquisition Says it will Redeem & Dissolve |url=https://news.spacconference.com/2023/05/10/jeb-bushs-jackson-acquisition-says-it-will-redeem-dissolve/ |website=SPAC News |access-date=13 March 2026 |date=May 10, 2023}}</ref> The [[Internal Revenue Service]] sued Jackson Investment Group{{nbsp}}LLC in 2024 for underreporting $72{{nbsp}}million in taxable income for 2019, seeking $38{{nbsp}}million. The IRS made the determination after disallowing expenses related to several philanthropic ventures.<ref name="bt-navera-24">{{cite news |last1=Navera |first1=Tristan |title=Atlanta Business’ Film, Real Estate Deductions Draw IRS Lawsuit |url=https://news.bloombergtax.com/daily-tax-report/atlanta-business-film-real-estate-deductions-draw-irs-lawsuit |work=Bloomberg Tax |date=June 21, 2024}}</ref>

===Jackson Healthcare===
In 2000, he founded Jackson Healthcare, which provides healthcare staffing and workforce services.<ref name="JH-about">{{cite web |title=About |website=Jackson Healthcare |url=https://jacksonhealthcare.com/about/ |access-date=February 7, 2026}}</ref> The company opened a new $100{{nbsp}}million campus in March 2019, designed by architectural firm Rule Joy Trammell Rubio (RJTR) and styled after an Italian piazza.<ref name="am-2019"/><ref name="gabiz-24"/><ref name="jh-rjtr">{{cite web |title=Jackson Healthcare |url=https://rjtrdesign.com/jackson-healthcare-interiors/ |publisher=RJTR |access-date=13 March 2026}}</ref> Jackson Healthcare earned nearly $1{{nbsp}}billion from 2020 through 2026 {{emdash}} most during the [[COVID-19 pandemic]] {{emdash}} from various Georgia agencies through state contracts.<ref name="wabe-grapevine-26">{{cite news |last1=Grapevine |first1=Rebecca |title=A health care executive is running for Ga. governor. His company has had about $1B in state contracts |url=https://www.wabe.org/a-health-care-executive-is-running-for-ga-governor-his-company-has-had-about-1b-in-state-contracts/ |work=WABE |date=March 2, 2026}}</ref> 

In 2021, Jackson Healthcare purchased USAntibiotics, an antibiotics manufacturer in [[Bristol, Tennessee]].<ref name="AP-announcement" /><ref name="gabiz-24"/> Jackson Healthcare acquired [[Omaha]]-based LRS{{nbsp}}Healthcare in May{{nbsp}}2023.<ref name="bw-lrs-23">{{cite web |title=Jackson Healthcare to Acquire LRS Healthcare |url=https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20230509005785/en/Jackson-Healthcare-to-Acquire-LRS-Healthcare |publisher=Business Wire |access-date=11 February 2026 |date=May 9, 2023}}</ref> Jackson Healthcare is privately-held and reports more than $3{{nbsp}}billion in annual revenue.<ref name="AP-announcement" /> [[John Bardis]], Jeb Bush, and [[Tom Price (American politician)|Tom Price]] are on the advisory board.<ref name="jh-jeb-bush">{{cite web |title=Governor Jeb Bush |url=https://jacksonhealthcare.com/team-members/governor-jeb-bush/ |website=Jackson Healthcare |access-date=13 March 2026}}</ref><ref name="ap-amy-26">{{cite news |last1=Amy |first1=Jeff |title=Burt Jones was the inevitable Republican nominee for Georgia governor. Then Rick Jackson showed up |url=https://apnews.com/article/rick-jackson-georgia-governor-burt-jones-trump-4c1789c599857e220180068e26de9199 |work=[[Associated Press]] |date=March 14, 2026}}</ref>

==Political career==
In the 2010s, Jackson funded an unsuccessful overhaul of Georgia medical malpractice claims, and wanted to privatize the state's foster care system. He successfully increased support for foster children. He has financially supported many [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican]] politicians, contributing millions to the [[Republican National Committee]], state parties, and candidates. In December 2025, he gave $1{{nbsp}}million to [[Make America Great Again Inc.|Make America Great Again{{nbsp}}Inc.]], a super PAC that acts as [[Donald Trump]]'s political organization.<ref name="ap-amy-26"/>

===2026 Georgia gubernatorial election===
{{main|2026 Georgia gubernatorial election}}
In February 2026, Jackson announced his candidacy for the Republican nomination for governor of Georgia and said he would self-fund a major portion of his campaign.<ref name="AP-announcement" /><ref name="Ballotpedia">{{cite web |title=Rick Jackson (Georgia) |website=Ballotpedia |url=https://ballotpedia.org/Rick_Jackson_(Georgia) |access-date=February 7, 2026}}</ref><ref name="gr-rocha-26">{{cite news |last1=Rocha |first1=Alander |title=A little known candidate shakes up GOP primary for Georgia governor |url=https://georgiarecorder.com/2026/02/07/a-little-known-candidate-shakes-up-gop-primary-for-georgia-governor/ |work=[[Georgia Recorder]] |date=February 7, 2026}}</ref><ref name="cbs-2-26">{{cite news |title=Georgia businessman Rick Jackson announces run for governor |url=https://www.cbsnews.com/atlanta/news/georgia-businessman-rick-jackson-announces-run-for-governor/ |work=[[CBS News]] |date=February 3, 2026}}</ref> He wants to freeze Georgia's property tax and cut the state's income tax in half. Describing his stance on [[diversity, equity, and inclusion]] (DEI) in an interview with [[WSB-TV]], Jackson said, "I believe in diversity and inclusion. I don't believe in equity. I don't believe in equal outcomes, period," calling the latter "communism".<ref name="wsb-estevez-26">{{cite news |last1=Estevez |first1=Jorge |title=Billionaire Rick Jackson Details Plan For Georgia If He's Elected Governor |url=https://www.wsbtv.com/news/local/atlanta/billionaire-rick-jackson-details-plan-georgia-if-hes-elected-governor/JXTFJGLCMJFDZHTJP7PR7PTVUM/ |access-date=13 March 2026 |work=[[WSB-TV]] |date=March 11, 2026}}</ref> 

He was endorsed by Georgia Attorney General [[Chris Carr (politician)|Chris Carr]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Carr backs businessman Rick Jackson in Georgia’s GOP runoff for governor | website=WABE |url=https://www.wabe.org/carr-backs-businessman-rick-jackson-in-georgias-gop-runoff-for-governor/ |date=May 27, 2026}}</ref>

On May{{nbsp}}19, 2026, Jackson received 32.5% of the vote in the Republican primary. Jackson defeated [[Burt Jones]] in a Republican runoff election on June{{nbsp}}16, 2026. Jones had been endorsed by President Trump and Governor [[Brian Kemp]]. Since winning the Republican nomination, both Trump and Kemp have supported Jackson. He will face Democratic nominee [[Keisha Lance Bottoms]] in the 2026 Georgia gubernatorial election.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Rojas |first=Rick |date=2026-06-17 |title=Rick Jackson, a Billionaire, Wins G.O.P. Runoff for Georgia Governor |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2026/06/16/us/elections/georgia-governor-runoff-republican.html |access-date=2026-06-17 |work=The New York Times |language=en-US |issn=0362-4331}}</ref>

==Personal life==
Jackson lives in [[Cumming, Georgia]], in a {{convert|47000|sqft|sqm|adj=on}} mansion.<ref name="ajc-quinn-2011">{{cite news |last1=Quinn |first1=Christopher |title=Million-dollar homes sell — at a slower pace |url=https://www.ajc.com/lifestyles/home--garden/million-dollar-homes-sell-slower-pace/pplqiv6JRUdXjSOMmDXcQP/ |work=[[The Atlanta Journal-Constitution]] |date=November 2, 2011}}</ref> He and his wife Melody ({{nee|Moore}}) have three children.<ref name="gabiz-24">{{cite news |last1=Ritterbusch |first1=Katja |title=Fostering Success |url=https://jacksonhealthcare.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/GABiz_Fall2024_FollowTheLeader.pdf |work=GaBiz |date=Fall 2024}}</ref> One of their sons is president of Jackson Healthcare, and another son runs the Jackson Family Foundation.<ref name="am-2019">{{cite news |last1=Grochowski |first1=Julia |title=Jackson Healthcare unveils $100 million expansion |url=https://www.appenmedia.com/business/jackson-healthcare-unveils-100-million-expansion/article_e50c0b2a-4481-11e9-8861-bbad25897bcf.html |work=Appen Media |date=March 11, 2019}}</ref><ref name="gr-rocha-26"/>

Jackson co-produced the film ''[[90 Minutes in Heaven (film)|90{{nbsp}}Minutes in Heaven]]'' (2015).<ref name="gabiz-24"/> He is a Christian.<ref name="ap-amy-26"/>

